    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "A grey cat stares quietly at a nearby orange cat. She "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "squints",
            "zh": "眯"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " her eyes, flattens her ears, and licks her lips. The orange cat stares back, "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "wrinkles",
            "zh": "皱"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " his nose, and pulls back his "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "whiskers",
            "zh": "胡须"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Cat people know what’s about to go down: a "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b1",
            "number": 1
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Claws",
            "zh": "爪子"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " may soon pop out and fur fly."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Cats make more faces than they do at each other, "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b2",
            "number": 2
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Researchers have discovered that cats have nearly 300 facial expressions. Their study was published in the journal "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "Behavioural Processes"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Many people still consider cats to be "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "nonsocial",
            "ref": "v-nonsocial"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ",” says Daniel Mills, an animal expert at the University of Lincoln. This new study suggests that that’s not "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b3",
            "number": 3
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Cats do often enjoy being "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b4",
            "number": 4
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". However, they can form friendships with other cats in people’s homes or on the street. "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Feral",
            "zh": "野生的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " cats can even live in groups of thousands."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Lauren Scott is a cat person. She has long wondered how cats communicate with one another. In 2021, she was studying at the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA). The campus was just minutes away from a "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b5",
            "number": 5
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " café. There, Scott recorded 194 minutes of cats’ facial expressions on video. She and evolutionary psychologist "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "evolutionary psychologist",
            "zh": "进化心理学家"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " Brittany Florkiewicz then studied all their facial muscle "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b6",
            "number": 6
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The pair identified 276 distinct cat facial expressions directed toward other cats. Each expression combined about four of the 26 "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b7",
            "number": 7
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " movements. These included parted lips, "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "blinks",
            "ref": "v-blink"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", and different ear positions. In the study, they found that most of the cats’ expressions were either obviously friendly (45%) or "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "aggressive",
            "ref": "v-aggressive"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " (37%). The rest could fall into "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b8",
            "number": 8
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " groups."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "One day, the study might be used to design an app to help cat "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b9",
            "number": 9
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " better understand their pets. The findings may also help people choose cats that are more likely to get along with their existing "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b10",
            "number": 10
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". If those fur babies are dogs, however, this may not work. Scientists don’t yet know whether dogs also understand the meanings of catty facial expressions."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
